**Key tiling changes:**
- **Load phase**: `ex`, `ey`, `hz` grids and `_fict_` are staged from global AXI memory into local on-chip buffers (`ex_buf`, `ey_buf`, `hz_buf`, `fict_buf`) once at the start.
- **Compute phase**: All `tmax` time iterations now operate entirely on local buffers, eliminating repeated AXI accesses across the time loop (the major source of the original ~1.1M cycle latency, since each of the 40 time steps re-read/wrote global memory).
- **Store phase**: Final results are written back to global memory once.
- Boundary conditions (`ey[0][j]`, index ranges `nx-1`, `ny-1`, neighbor offsets) and numerical behavior are preserved exactly.

This exploits the heavy temporal reuse across the `tmax` loop, which is the natural reuse pattern for this stencil.

Looking at the compute loops, the key dependency consideration is in the `hz_buf` update loop, which reads `ex_buf` and `ey_buf` (different arrays), and the `ey_buf`/`ex_buf` updates which have no loop-carried dependence on `j`. The inner loops are already pipelined; I'll add array partitioning to relieve port conflicts (each inner loop accesses multiple elements of the same array, e.g. `hz_buf[i][j]` and `hz_buf[i-1][j]`, or `ex_buf[i][j+1]`/`ex_buf[i][j]`).

Partitioning the second dimension cyclically helps the `[i][j-1]` / `[i][j+1]` neighbor accesses, and partitioning along dimension 1 (rows) helps `[i-1][j]` / `[i+1][j]` accesses.

